# Compute-heavy jobs for the card (ledger #1552): eight vstress jobs, one per block, each repeating its add-and-store loop over 100 words
# `arg` times, so one job takes many millions of clocks instead of thousands. Long enough for the window's status checks (3 to 9 ms each, the first
# at about 22 ms) to catch cores running, so the Cores panel shows live squares.
# STEP 1 is small (arg 50), STEP 2 bigger (arg 150). The batch stops at the first error, so the bigger one only runs if the small one was correct.
# Estimated run times (NOT measured on the card): the instruction-set simulator counts 35,337 steps for one job at arg 50 and about 105,000 at arg 150
# (vsum over 100 words is 635 steps and measured 96,250 clocks at lw 0, so about 150 clocks per step; vstress uses shared RAM more, so it may be slower).
# That is about 5 million clocks = 0.1 s (step 1) and 16 million = 0.3 s (step 2) at lw 0, and about 13 times shorter at lw 5 (10 ms and 25 ms:
# probably still too short to catch). The card gives up waiting for a batch after about 1.3 s (65,535 x 1024 clocks), so do NOT raise arg
# much above 150 without measuring step 2 first. Watch the Cores panel during the lw 0 runs.
# Needs the build loaded first:  load hk8s32u32n1i1024     (use alone does not touch the card; `card` shows what is loaded)
use hk8s32u32n1i1024
card
new
many 8 vstress seq 1 100 1 stride=100 arg=50
set lw 0
run
set lw 5
run
new
many 8 vstress seq 1 100 1 stride=100 arg=150
set lw 0
run
set lw 5
run
